<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of the European Union</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Council of Ministers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> is the institution in which government ministers of the member states meet to adopt EU laws, usually jointly with the European Parliament, and to coordinate policy. Its presidency rotates among the member states every six months, and the country holding it chairs most meetings and sets priorities. It is distinct from the European Council of heads of government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union.jpg" alt="Council of the European Union" width="320" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: European Union · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council_of_the_EU_and_European_Council.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Council first appeared in the European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC) as the Special Council of Ministers, set up to counterbalance the High Authority, the supranational executive that later became the Commission. Its powers were limited: coal and steel matters lay in the Authority's domain, and the Council's consent was needed only for decisions outside them. The Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> of 1957 established two new communities, the European Atomic Energy Community and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, each with a Council of its own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, boycotted all Council meetings over disagreements about the Commission's agriculture proposals, among other things. This empty chair crisis halted the Council's work until the Luxembourg compromise resolved it the following year. The Merger Treaty of 1967 merged the two Councils into a single Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a>, which took the name Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 1993, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Maastricht Treaty</a> created the Union.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> added intergovernmental elements, but Parliament and Commission gained strength at the same time. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> abolished the pillars, gave Parliament further powers and merged the Council's High Representative with the Commission's foreign policy head, who chairs the Foreign Affairs Council in place of the rotating presidency. It also made the European Council a separate institution with a permanent president. The development of the Council has been marked by the rise of Parliament, with which it has had to share legislative power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Council is one of two legislative bodies of the Union, with the European Parliament. Together they amend, approve or veto proposals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a>, which holds the right of initiative, and they share the budgetary power. Most laws now pass under the ordinary legislative procedure, which needs the consent of both bodies. Ministers of the member states meet in different configurations according to subject.</p><p>Most decisions are taken by qualified majority: at least 55 per cent of member states, at least 15 of them, representing at least 65 per cent of the population. Foreign and security policy generally requires unanimity. The presidency rotates every six months among the member states, whose government sets the agenda and chairs meetings, and from 2007 three successive presidencies have shared a common programme.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of the European Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It held the presidency of the <b>Council of the European Union</b> in 2011 and 2024.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the protectorate from 1883–1884, emperors kept their court, rituals and mandarins, yet French residents controlled finances and appointments, and from 1897 a French official presided over the <b>Council of Ministers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A <b>Council of Ministers</b> runs the federal administration, and the Federal National Council, an advisory body of 40 members, has had half its seats filled since 2006 by an electoral college whose members are chosen by the rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>As different versions of the melody came into use, the government set up a committee in 1956 to fix an official one; the present version was proposed on 16 July 1957 and approved by the <b>Council of Ministers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-portuguesa">A Portuguesa</a></li><li><blockquote>In Cyprus, an anthem written for the Republic by the Cypriot composer Solon Michaelides and shared with the Turkish Cypriots was abandoned in 1963 during intercommunal conflicts, and in 1966 the <b>Council of Ministers</b> adopted the &quot;Hymn to Liberty&quot; as the national anthem of Cyprus.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hymn-to-liberty">Hymn to Liberty</a></li></ul></section>
